<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Adaptive-icon background layer: the canvas drawn as a disc instead of a full
  bleed, so Flick is a circle by choice rather than whatever shape the launcher's
  mask happens to be.

  An adaptive layer is 108 units. The system keeps the outer 18 per side for
  parallax, so a mask spans at most the central 72 — r=36 from (54,54) at its
  edge midpoints — but only the 66-unit safe circle, r=33, is guaranteed to
  survive every mask.

  The disc is r=33, not r=36. At 36 its edge is tangent to every stock mask at
  those four midpoints, with no tolerance at all: a launcher that scales the
  layer before masking (One UI does this to third-party icons), a mask drawn a
  hair inside 72, or plain antialiasing along that shared boundary each cut the
  disc flat there, and it reads as a squircle again. 33 is the largest radius
  lying wholly inside the guaranteed circle, so no mask can reach the edge.

  The 3 units cost diameter, not shape: under a circular mask the icon is 91.7%
  the width of a full-bleed neighbour. They do not cost a ring of wallpaper —
  nothing draws the mask boundary, so what is left is simply a smaller circle.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <path
        android:fillColor="@color/ic_launcher_background"
        android:pathData="M54,21a33,33 0 1 1 0,66a33,33 0 1 1 0,-66z" />

</vector>
